At the Regional level, the literacy rates are: Eastern Region – 47 .5 percent; Northern Region 42.2 percent;
Southern Region -  45.2 percent and Western Area -  74.5 percent.
 1.4 Economic characteristics
The results show that 68.6 percent of the working age population (15-64 years) are economically active,
known also as the labour force (employed and unemployed), and 31.4 percent are non-economically active.
Employment Rate
Figure 1.8 shows that 91.4 percent of the labour force are employed, and 8.6 percent are unemployed. The
employment rate is almost the same for male and female, 91.5 percent and 91.3 percent, respectively. At the
regional level, the employment rates are: Eastern Region -  94.1 percent, Northern Region -  93.5 percent,
Southern Region -  92.1 percent and Western Area – 84.7 percent.
Figure 1.8: Employment and Unemployment Rates
Employment Sector
Figure 1.9 shows that the self-employment sector is the largest employer in the country, accounting for 83.9
percent, followed by public sector - 6.2 percent; private sector - 4.0 percent; family member – 3.8 percent;
NGOs/Embassy/International Organisations -  1.4 percent and others - 0.6 percent.
Figure 1.9: Employment sector
Industry
Figure 1.10 reveals that agriculture - including forestry and fishing, crop farming, and animal production - is
the largest industrial sector, employing 59.2 percent of the employed population. The next major industry is
services at 31.1 percent, followed by industrial (manufacturing, utilities, construction and mining) accounting
for 9.6 percent.
Even though agriculture is the most predominant industry, data shows some variations in the proportions at
the regional level. As shown by figure 1.10, of those engaged in agriculture, 25.8 percent are in the Northern
region, 17 .1 percent are in the Eastern region and 14.6 percent are in the Southern region. Western area,
however, accounts for 15.1 percent of the employed population engaged in services.
