1.5 other socioeconomic characteristics
Birth Registration
The data shows that out of the total household population, 43.1 percent have birth certificates, 45.1 percent for
males and 41.1 percent for females. In general, possession of birth certificate decreases with age; 65.1 percent of
0-4 years have birth certificate compared to 8.2 percent of population 75 years and older.
Immunization
The data indicates that out of 1,180,795 children aged 0-5 years, 54.0 percent are fully immunised, 38.6 percent
partially immunised and 1.4 percent were never immunized at the national level. The data shows almost no
difference between boys and girls in immunisation status.
Tobacco and Alcohol Intake
The data shows that out of the 5,030,016 population 10 years and over, 9.1 percent took tobacco only, 3.5 percent
took alcohol only, 2.6 percent took both tobacco and alcohol and 83.4 percent took neither tobacco or alcohol.
For 1.5 percent, the respondent did not know.  The data shows also that 12.7 percent of the male population took
tobacco only, while 4.5 percent took both tobacco and alcohol. For female population, 5.6 percent took tobacco
only and 0.8 percent took both tobacco and alcohol.
Internet Access and Use
The data shows that out of the population 10 year and over, 651,826 people have access to internet, that is 13.0
percent nationwide. Male population has a slightly higher access (16.0) than female population (10.0%).Of those
who have access to internet, 72.9 percent have access at home, 5.8 in the office, 2.8 percent from Internet café,
and 5.8 from other sources, whilst 12.7 percent did not use Internet during the reference period (past one week
preceding the census).
1.6 Ebola Socioeconomic impacts
Sierra Leone was hit by an unprecedented outbreak of Ebola Viral Disease (EVD) for 18 months, May 2014 –
November 2015. Thousands of people were affected by the disease, which also had a devastating impact on
the country’s economy and its health system. The 2015 PHC collected a wide range of data on the EVD socio-
economic impacts. This section presents a brief summary of the results.
Households with EVD Cases
The results show that out of 1,265,468 households nationwide, 6,951 reported to have at least one member
confirmed positive of contracting EVD, representing 3,765 households in the rural areas and 3,186 in the urban
areas. At the regional level, 22.0 percent of the affected households live in the Eastern region, 48.4 percent in the
Northern region, 8.0 percent in the Southern region and 21.6 percent in the Western Area.
Persons confirmed positive of EVD
At the national level, 13,575 persons were reported to have contracted the EVD. At the regional level, 2,983 persons
were reported to have contracted the disease in the Eastern region, 6,566 persons in the Northern region, 1,090
in the Southern region and 2,936 in the Western Area.
orphans due to EVD
The data reveals that 6,499 children nationwide below the age of 18 years have lost at least one biological
parent due to the EVD outbreak. The number of reported orphaned children due to EVD per region are: 1,657
in the Eastern region, 2,922 in the Northern region, 873 in the Southern region and 1,047 in the Western Area.
II.  HoUSInG CHARACTERISTICS
2.1 Housing conditions
Stock of houses
The 2015 PHC results reveal that the total stock of houses in the country is 801,417. The proportion of houses
in rural areas (60.6%) is higher than that in urban areas (39.4%). The regional distribution shows that Eastern
region counts for 21.8 percent of the stock of houses, Northern region 34.3 percent, Southern region 22.7
percent and Western Area 21.1 percent.
The population per house is 8.8 persons and ranges from a low of 7.9 persons in the Southern region to a high
of 9.4 persons in the Eastern region. On average, Sierra Leone has 1.6 households per house and ranges from
a low of 1.4 in the Southern region to a high of 1.9 in the Western Area.
